  <div id="calc-orm-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How We Calculate This</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        There's no single "most accurate" formula for estimating a one-rep max from a submaximal set — every
        equation is a population-average approximation, so we show you two of the most widely used ones side by
        side instead of picking a favorite.
        <br /><br />
        The <strong>Epley formula</strong> (1RM = weight × (1 + reps/30)) is the most widely used approximation
        in gyms and coaching, originally published as a poundage chart rather than in a peer-reviewed journal.
        <br /><br />
        The <strong>Brzycki formula</strong> (1RM = weight × 36 ÷ (37 − reps)) comes from a peer-reviewed paper
        and tends to estimate slightly lower than Epley at higher rep counts. It becomes mathematically
        undefined at 37 reps or more (the denominator hits zero), so it isn't usable outside a realistic
        strength-testing rep range.
        <br /><br />
        Both formulas are most reliable for sets of 1–10 reps — accuracy drops the higher your rep count goes,
        since fatigue and muscular endurance start to matter more than raw strength. For the most reliable
        estimate, use a recent set of 1–6 reps taken close to failure.
        <br /><br />
        Once you have an estimate, the <strong>Training Load Table</strong> below it converts that number into
        practical training weights using the NSCA's percentage-of-1RM chart — so you can see what to load on the
        bar for a strength-focused set of 3 reps versus a hypertrophy-focused set of 8, without doing the math
        yourself.
      </p>
      <strong>Source:</strong> Epley, B. (1985). <em>Poundage chart.</em> In <em>Boyd Epley Workout.</em> Lincoln, NE: Body Enterprises.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Source:</strong> Brzycki, M. (1993). <em>Strength testing—Predicting a one-rep max from
      reps-to-fatigue.</em> Journal of Physical Education, Recreation &amp; Dance, 64(1), 88–90.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/07303084.1993.10606684" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the Brzycki study →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Source:</strong> Landers, J. (1984). <em>Maximum based on reps.</em> NSCA Journal, 6(6), 60–61.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://www.nsca.com/contentassets/61d813865e264c6e852cadfe247eae52/nsca_training_load_chart.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the NSCA training load chart →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

